      transcript:
        "Ever wondered how to fix a broken PVC pipe without calling a plumber? Today, I\u2019ll show you step-by-step how to repair a damaged 2-inch PVC pipe like a pro. \n\nFirst, shut off the main water supply and drain the pipes. Make sure the area around the damaged pipe is clear and safe to work in. This should take about 20 to 30 minutes.\n\nNext, mark the damaged section. Use a PVC cutter or a fine-tooth saw to cut out the bad piece. Clean the ends of the remaining pipe\u2014make sure your cuts are straight and smooth, with no rough edges. This step usually takes 30 to 45 minutes.\n\nNow, dry-fit your new PVC pipe and couplings to check the fit. Once you\u2019re sure everything lines up, apply PVC primer to the pipe ends and inside the couplings. Then, brush on the PVC cement. Quickly assemble the joints, giving them a little twist to spread the glue. Hold them together for a few seconds, then let the joints cure for at least 30 minutes before turning the water back on. This part takes about an hour to an hour and a half.\n\nIf you liked this video, I appreciate the like and follow. If you're a contractor, come check out Contractor Plus. We've built the ultimate operating system for build and service contractors. Thanks for watching!",
