      "@type": "VideoObject",
      name: "How to Safely Remove Asbestos: Step-by-Step Guide",
      description:
        "Calculate materials & costs to asbestos removal at https://contractorplus.app/resources/contractor-costs/asbestos-removal/national-average\n\nIn this video, we break down the complete process of safe asbestos removal for homeowners and contractors. Learn how to prepare your site, protect yourself with the right gear, and remove asbestos-containing materials without spreading dangerous fibers. We cover essential materials like 6-mil polyethylene sheeting, HEPA vacuums, and proper waste disposal bags. You'll also see how to set up containment, use personal protective equipment, and perform final air quality checks. Whether you're planning a DIY project or managing a crew, these steps will help you stay safe and compliant. Like and follow for more expert construction tips, and visit Contractor Plus for the best contractor tools and resources!",
      thumbnailUrl:
        "https://contractor-plus-website.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com/og-images/3574f563-4e57-4f3a-978a-df6f179ea115.jpg",
      uploadDate: "2025-08-04",
      duration: "PT2M8S",
      contentUrl:
        "https://contractor-plus-website.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com/reshub-how-to-vids/asbestos-removal.mp4",
      embedUrl:
        "https://contractorplus.app/resources/contractor-costs/asbestos-removal/national-average",
      transcript:
        "Did you know asbestos removal can be safe and affordable if you follow the right steps? In this video, you'll learn exactly how to remove asbestos from your property, step by step. \n\nFirst, prepare the site. Seal off the work area with 6-mil plastic sheeting, use duct tape to secure all seams, and turn off any HVAC systems. Post warning signs to keep people out.\n\nNext, protect yourself. Put on disposable coveralls, gloves, and a respirator with P100 filters. Set up negative air machines and a decontamination area to keep asbestos fibers from spreading.\n\nNow, it's time to remove the asbestos. Wet the material to keep dust down. Carefully remove it, double-bag all debris in 6-mil asbestos waste bags, and seal them tight. Always handle waste gently to avoid releasing fibers.\n\nAfter removal, clean every surface with a HEPA vacuum. Take down the plastic barriers and run air quality tests to make sure the area is safe.\n\nIf you liked this video, I appreciate the like and follow. If you're a contractor, come check out Contractor Plus. We've built the ultimate operating system for build and service contractors. Thanks for watching!",
