      embedUrl:
        "https://contractorplus.app/resources/contractor-costs/sprinkler-winterization/national-average",
      transcript:
        "Did you know one missed step in winterizing your sprinklers can cost you hundreds in repairs? Today, I\u2019ll show you how to winterize your sprinkler system the right way, so you avoid frozen pipes and costly damage. \n\nFirst, shut off the main water supply to your sprinklers. Open all manual drain valves to relieve any pressure in the lines. This step takes about 10 minutes but is crucial to prevent water from getting trapped.\n\nNext, it\u2019s time to blow out the lines. Rent an air compressor and connect it to the system\u2019s blow-out port using quick connect fittings and Teflon tape for a tight seal. Wear protective gloves. Work through each sprinkler zone one at a time, blowing air through until no water comes out. Make sure you use the right air pressure for your system\u2014check your manual if you\u2019re unsure. This step usually takes about an hour to an hour and a half.\n\nFinally, insulate any exposed pipes, backflow preventers, and valves with foam covers or insulation tape. Close all drain valves, and set your sprinkler controller to off or rain mode for the winter.\n\nIf you liked this video, I appreciate the like and follow. If you're a contractor, come check out Contractor Plus. We've built the ultimate operating system for build and service contractors. Thanks for watching!",
