      embedUrl:
        "https://contractorplus.app/resources/contractor-costs/garage-door-spring-replacement/national-average",
      transcript:
        "Did you know a broken garage door spring can trap your car and risk your safety? Today, I\u2019ll show you exactly how to replace garage door springs step by step. \n\nFirst, safety is key. Disconnect your garage door opener and clamp the door in place so it can\u2019t move. Put on safety glasses and gloves. Gather your new torsion springs, winding bars, center and end bearings, torsion cables, and all hardware.\n\nNext, carefully release the tension from the old springs using winding bars. Never use screwdrivers\u2014they can slip. Loosen the set screws, unwind the springs, and remove them. Check the bearings and cables for wear. Replace any damaged parts.\n\nNow, install the new springs. Slide them onto the torsion bar, secure the center and end bearings, and attach the cables. Use winding bars to add the right number of turns\u2014follow the manufacturer\u2019s chart. Tighten all set screws. Remove the clamps, reconnect the opener, and test the door. It should open and close smoothly and stay balanced halfway up.\n\nIf you liked this video, I appreciate the like and follow. If you're a contractor, come check out Contractor Plus. We've built the ultimate operating system for build and service contractors. Thanks for watching!",
