      "@type": "VideoObject",
      name: "How to Install a French Drain: Step-by-Step Guide",
      description:
        "Calculate materials & costs to french drain installation at https://contractorplus.app/resources/contractor-costs/french-drain-installation/national-average\n\nIn this video, we walk you through the complete process of installing a French drain to solve drainage problems around your home or property. You'll learn how to plan and mark your trench, dig with the right slope, lay the perforated pipe and gravel, and finish the job for lasting results. We cover the essential materials, tools, and time needed for each step, so you can tackle this project with confidence. Whether you're a homeowner or a contractor, these clear instructions will help you prevent water damage and keep your property dry. Like and follow for more practical construction tips, and let us know your questions or experiences in the comments!",
      thumbnailUrl:
        "https://contractor-plus-website.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com/og-images/33d2d008-edee-4645-ae16-f9ca3c49207c.jpg",
      uploadDate: "2025-08-04",
      duration: "PT2M14S",
      contentUrl:
        "https://contractor-plus-website.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com/reshub-how-to-vids/french-drain-installation.mp4",
      embedUrl:
        "https://contractorplus.app/resources/contractor-costs/french-drain-installation/national-average",
      transcript:
        "Tired of water pooling in your yard or basement? A French drain could be your solution. In this video, you'll learn exactly how to install a French drain, step by step, so you can keep your property dry and protected.\n\nFirst, plan and mark your drain route. Walk your property, find the problem spots, and use spray paint or string to mark a trench line that leads water away from your home. This takes about 2 to 3 hours but is key for good drainage.\n\nNext, it's time to dig. Excavate a trench along your marked line, about 18 inches deep and 12 inches wide. Make sure the trench slopes at least 1 inch for every 10 feet, so water flows the right way. Remove all soil and debris to keep your work area safe. This step usually takes 8 to 10 hours for 100 feet.\n\nNow, lay the drain system. Start with a few inches of \u00be-inch clean gravel at the bottom. Place a 4-inch perforated drain pipe on top, holes facing down. Wrap the pipe in geotextile fabric to keep out dirt and roots. Add pipe fittings and a pop-up emitter at the outlet if needed. This part takes 6 to 8 hours.\n\nFinally, backfill and finish. Cover the pipe with more gravel, fold the fabric over, then fill the trench with soil. Restore your lawn or landscaping and clean up the site. This last step takes 8 to 10 hours.\n\nIf you liked this video, I appreciate the like and follow. If you're a contractor, come check out Contractor Plus. We've built the ultimate operating system for build and service contractors. Thanks for watching!",
