      name: "Drywall Mudding: Step-by-Step Guide for Smooth Walls",
      description:
        "Calculate materials & costs to drywall mudding at https://contractorplus.app/resources/contractor-costs/drywall-mudding/national-average\n\nIn this video, we break down the drywall mudding process so you can achieve flawless, paint-ready walls. Learn exactly what materials you need for a 1,000 sq ft project, including joint compound, tape, corner bead, and more. We\u2019ll walk you through each step: prepping the surface, applying and feathering coats of mud, and sanding for a smooth finish. Whether you\u2019re a DIY homeowner or a contractor, these clear instructions will help you get professional results. Have questions or tips? Drop them in the comments! Don\u2019t forget to like and follow for more how-to videos, and check out Contractor Plus for the best contractor tools and resources.",
      thumbnailUrl:
        "https://contractor-plus-website.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com/og-images/45148286-1b1a-4e36-a37e-29f82a88a900.jpg",
      uploadDate: "2025-08-04",
      duration: "PT2M35S",
      contentUrl:
        "https://contractor-plus-website.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com/reshub-how-to-vids/drywall-mudding.mp4",
      embedUrl:
        "https://contractorplus.app/resources/contractor-costs/drywall-mudding/national-average",
      transcript:
        "Want perfectly smooth walls? Here\u2019s how to master drywall mudding, step by step.\n\nIn this video, you\u2019ll learn how to prep, mud, and finish drywall like a pro.\n\nFirst, get your materials: joint compound, paper tape, corner bead, sanding sponges, and plastic sheeting. For 1,000 square feet, you\u2019ll need about two 5-gallon buckets of joint compound, two rolls of tape, ten corner beads, four sanding sponges, and a roll of plastic sheeting to protect your space.\n\nStart with surface preparation. Clean all drywall seams and remove dust. Apply drywall tape to every joint and corner. This helps the mud stick and prevents cracks.\n\nNext, apply the first coat of joint compound over the taped seams and screw holes using a drywall knife. Let it dry completely\u2014don\u2019t rush this step.\n\nOnce dry, add a second, wider coat. Feather the edges so the mud blends smoothly into the wall. Let it dry again.\n\nFor the final coat, apply a thin layer and let it dry. Then, sand all mudded areas with a sanding sponge until smooth. Wipe down the walls to remove dust. Now you\u2019re ready for paint!\n\nIf you liked this video, I appreciate the like and follow. If you're a contractor, come check out Contractor Plus. We've built the ultimate operating system for build and service contractors. Thanks for watching!",
