      transcript:
        "Is your basement at risk of flooding every time it rains? Today, I\u2019ll show you how to install a sump pump in your basement, step by step. \n\nFirst, clear the work area and mark where your sump pit will go. Use a hammer drill or jackhammer to break up the concrete, then dig a pit about 18 inches wide and 22 inches deep. \n\nNext, set the sump basin in the hole. Make sure it\u2019s level and stable. If you have drain tile, connect it now so water flows into the basin. Add gravel around the basin for drainage. \n\nNow, place the sump pump inside the basin. Attach a check valve to the pump\u2019s outlet, then connect your 1-1/2 inch PVC discharge pipe. Run the pipe up and out of the house, making sure it drains water away from your foundation. Seal around the basin with concrete patch. \n\nFinally, plug the pump into a GFCI outlet or have an electrician hardwire it. Fill the basin with water to test the pump and check for leaks. \n\nIf you liked this video, I appreciate the like and follow. If you're a contractor, come check out Contractor Plus. We've built the ultimate operating system for build and service contractors. Thanks for watching!",
