        "https://contractorplus.app/resources/contractor-costs/pipe-relining/national-average",
      transcript:
        "Ever wondered how to fix old pipes without digging up your yard? Today, I\u2019ll show you exactly how pipe relining works, step by step. \n\nFirst, inspect your pipes using a CCTV camera. This helps you spot any damage and plan your work. Next, clean the inside of the pipe with high-pressure water or mechanical tools. Remove all debris and buildup\u2014this is key for a strong bond.\n\nNow, measure and cut your epoxy resin liner to match the pipe\u2019s length. Make sure the liner is fully saturated with resin. This creates a watertight seal inside the pipe.\n\nFor installation, insert the liner into the pipe using inversion or pull-in methods. Inflate or pressurize it so it molds tightly to the pipe walls. \n\nLet the liner cure for 12 to 15 hours. You can use hot water, steam, or just let it set with air. Once it\u2019s cured, deflate your equipment and do a final CCTV inspection to make sure everything looks good. Restore service, and you\u2019re done!\n\nIf you liked this video, I appreciate the like and follow. If you're a contractor, come check out Contractor Plus. We've built the ultimate operating system for build and service contractors. Thanks for watching!",
