        "https://contractorplus.app/resources/contractor-costs/install-sump-pump-discharge-pipe/national-average",
      transcript:
        "Ever wondered how to keep your basement dry with a proper sump pump discharge pipe? Today, I\u2019ll show you step-by-step how to install a discharge pipe that keeps water away from your home. \n\nFirst, plan your discharge route. Find the best path from your sump pump to a safe drainage spot, making sure you follow local codes and avoid your neighbor\u2019s property. This usually takes about 30 to 45 minutes.\n\nNext, measure and cut your 1.5-inch PVC pipe. Dry-fit the sections with elbow fittings and couplings to make sure everything lines up from the pump to the outside wall. This step should take about an hour.\n\nNow, drill a hole through your foundation or rim joist for the pipe to exit. Insert the pipe, seal around the opening with caulk or foam, and secure it to keep out leaks and pests.\n\nConnect the pipe to your sump pump. Use PVC cement and primer for tight joints, and add a check valve to stop water from flowing back. Secure the pipe with support straps along the wall or ground.\n\nFinally, test your system. Run water through the pump, check for leaks, and make sure the water flows away from your foundation. Adjust the pipe slope if needed.\n\nIf you liked this video, I appreciate the like and follow. If you're a contractor, come check out Contractor Plus. We've built the ultimate operating system for build and service contractors. Thanks for watching!",
