      transcript:
        "Want to avoid flood insurance headaches? You need an elevation certificate survey. \n\nIn this video, I\u2019ll show you exactly how the process works, step by step.\n\nFirst, a licensed land surveyor visits your property. They\u2019ll review your boundaries and set up special equipment at key points. This takes about an hour to an hour and a half.\n\nNext, the surveyor measures and records the elevation of your building\u2019s corners, the lowest floor, and the ground around it. These measurements must meet FEMA standards. This part usually takes two to two and a half hours.\n\nAfter collecting the data, the surveyor analyzes it and fills out the official elevation certificate. They double-check everything to make sure it meets local rules. This final step takes about two hours.\n\nCosts include the surveyor\u2019s fee, markers or stakes, and a processing fee. Expect to pay between $400 and $700 for the surveyor, plus small fees for materials and paperwork.\n\nIf you liked this video, I appreciate the like and follow. If you're a contractor, come check out Contractor Plus. We've built the ultimate operating system for build and service contractors. Thanks for watching!",
