      "@type": "VideoObject",
      name: "Radon Mitigation System Maintenance: Step-by-Step Guide",
      description:
        "Calculate materials & costs to radon mitigation system maintenance at https://contractorplus.app/resources/contractor-costs/radon-mitigation-system-maintenance/national-average\n\nIn this video, we walk you through the essential steps to maintain your radon mitigation system and keep your home safe from harmful radon gas. Learn how to inspect system components, clean and test airflow, check and replace filters, and monitor radon levels with easy-to-follow instructions. Whether you're a homeowner or a contractor, these tips will help you spot issues early and ensure your system is working efficiently. Have questions or want more tips? Drop a comment below, like, and follow for more helpful guides. Contractors\u2014discover how Contractor Plus can streamline your business today!",
      thumbnailUrl:
        "https://contractor-plus-website.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com/og-images/e5c994b5-a7eb-4395-81bb-5c0ebe6aa018.jpg",
      uploadDate: "2025-08-04",
      duration: "PT2M15S",
      contentUrl:
        "https://contractor-plus-website.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com/reshub-how-to-vids/radon-mitigation-system-maintenance.mp4",
      embedUrl:
        "https://contractorplus.app/resources/contractor-costs/radon-mitigation-system-maintenance/national-average",
      transcript:
        "Did you know a neglected radon mitigation system can put your health at risk? In this video, you'll learn how to maintain your radon mitigation system step by step, so your home stays safe. \n\nFirst, inspect all system components. Check the radon fan, PVC piping, and seals for any wear, cracks, or loose connections. Listen for odd noises or vibrations\u2014these can mean trouble. Tighten any loose fittings and replace damaged parts.\n\nNext, clean and test the airflow. Remove debris from vent pipes and the exterior exhaust. Use a manometer or airflow gauge to make sure the system is pulling air properly. If airflow is weak, check for blockages or a failing fan.\n\nNow, check and replace filters. Some systems have inline filters or screens. If they're dirty or clogged, swap them out and make sure the housings are sealed tight.\n\nFinally, monitor radon levels. Place a radon test kit or electronic monitor near the system. Record the readings to confirm the system is working. If levels are still high, you may need more repairs.\n\nIf you liked this video, I appreciate the like and follow. If you're a contractor, come check out Contractor Plus. We've built the ultimate operating system for build and service contractors. Thanks for watching!",
