      transcript:
        "Is your deck leaking where it meets the house? You might have a flashing problem\u2014and fixing it is easier than you think. In this video, you'll learn how to repair deck flashing step by step, so you can protect your home from water damage.\n\nFirst, inspect the area where your deck connects to the house. Look for rusted, bent, or missing flashing, and check for signs of water leaks. Clear the area and gather your tools: new flashing (aluminum or galvanized steel), flashing tape, galvanized nails or screws, exterior caulk, and basic hand tools.\n\nNext, carefully remove any siding or trim around the deck ledger. Take out the old, damaged flashing. Be gentle to avoid damaging the house wrap or wood underneath.\n\nNow, cut your new flashing to fit. Slide it behind the siding and over the ledger board, making sure it overlaps any joints by at least two inches. Secure it with galvanized nails or screws. Seal all edges and nail holes with exterior caulk to keep water out.\n\nReattach the siding or trim. Double-check all your seals, and test with a little water to make sure everything is watertight. Clean up your work area and dispose of old materials properly.\n\nIf you liked this video, I appreciate the like and follow. If you're a contractor, come check out Contractor Plus. We've built the ultimate operating system for build and service contractors. Thanks for watching!",
