        "https://contractorplus.app/resources/contractor-costs/pipe-replacement/national-average",
      transcript:
        "Ever wondered what it really takes to replace old pipes in your home? Today, I\u2019ll show you the step-by-step process for a safe and successful pipe replacement. \n\nFirst, always shut off your main water supply. Drain the lines and clear any obstacles so you can reach the pipes you need to replace. This usually takes about an hour.\n\nNext, remove the old pipes. Use a pipe cutter or saw to cut out damaged sections. Be sure to follow your local disposal rules for old materials. This step can take two to three hours.\n\nNow, it\u2019s time to install the new pipes. Measure and cut your PEX pipe to fit. Use elbows, tees, and couplings for turns and connections. Secure everything with pipe hangers or clamps, and don\u2019t forget to wrap the pipes with insulation. Use Teflon tape or joint compound on threaded connections to prevent leaks. This part takes four to five hours.\n\nFinally, turn the water back on and check for leaks. Inspect every joint and fitting. If you spot a leak, tighten the connection or reapply joint compound. Testing and inspection usually takes one to two hours.\n\nIf you liked this video, I appreciate the like and follow. If you're a contractor, come check out Contractor Plus. We've built the ultimate operating system for build and service contractors. Thanks for watching!",
