        "https://contractorplus.app/resources/contractor-costs/replace-polybutylene-pipes/national-average",
      transcript:
        "Did you know old polybutylene pipes can fail without warning and cause major water damage? In this video, you'll learn exactly how to replace them with modern PEX piping, step by step. \n\nFirst, assess your home\u2019s plumbing. Inspect all visible pipes and make a list of every polybutylene line you find. Plan your replacement route and get any permits you need. Gather your materials: about 400 feet of 1/2-inch PEX pipe, 40 fittings, 40 crimp rings, a manifold if needed, pipe insulation, drywall patch supplies, and shutoff valves.\n\nNext, shut off the main water supply and drain the system. Carefully remove all the old polybutylene pipes. Take your time to avoid damaging walls or ceilings.\n\nNow, install the new PEX pipes. Use the right fittings and crimp rings for secure joints. Follow local plumbing codes for alignment and support. If your system uses a manifold, install it now.\n\nFinally, turn the water back on and check for leaks at every joint. Test all fixtures. Patch up any drywall you opened and clean up the area.\n\nIf you liked this video, I appreciate the like and follow. If you're a contractor, come check out Contractor Plus. We've built the ultimate operating system for build and service contractors. Thanks for watching!",
