      name: "Industrial Demolition: Step-by-Step Guide & Cost Breakdown",
      description:
        "Calculate materials & costs to industrial demolition at https://contractorplus.app/resources/contractor-costs/industrial-demolition/national-average\n\nThinking about an industrial demolition project? This video breaks down every step, from site assessment and permitting to utility disconnection, structural demolition, and debris removal. Learn what equipment, materials, and labor hours you'll need for a safe and efficient job. We cover how to handle hazardous materials, control dust, and follow local safety and environmental rules. Whether you're a contractor or property owner, you'll get practical tips to plan your demolition and avoid costly mistakes. Like and follow for more expert construction guides, and check out Contractor Plus for the best contractor management tools!",
      thumbnailUrl:
        "https://contractor-plus-website.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com/og-images/c7c12f70-473e-4641-944f-d59ed15e0294.jpg",
      uploadDate: "2025-08-04",
      duration: "PT2M8S",
      contentUrl:
        "https://contractor-plus-website.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com/reshub-how-to-vids/industrial-demolition.mp4",
      embedUrl:
        "https://contractorplus.app/resources/contractor-costs/industrial-demolition/national-average",
      transcript:
        "Ever wondered what it really takes to demolish an industrial building, step by step? In this video, you'll learn the full process and what materials and labor you need for a safe, efficient industrial demolition. \n\nFirst, start with a site assessment. Walk the property, look for hazardous materials like asbestos or lead, and get all the permits you need from your local authorities. This can take 40 to 60 hours, but it's crucial for safety and legal compliance.\n\nNext, disconnect all utilities\u2014electricity, gas, and water. Make sure everything is shut off and safe before you start. Handle any hazardous materials according to your area's safety rules. This step usually takes 30 to 50 hours.\n\nNow, it's time for structural demolition. Use heavy equipment like excavators and loaders. You'll need to control dust with water or barriers, and keep the site stable as you work. This is the biggest part of the job, taking 200 to 220 hours.\n\nAfter demolition, sort and load debris into dumpsters. Recycle what you can, and dispose of hazardous waste properly. This cleanup phase takes 80 to 100 hours.\n\nFor materials, plan for heavy equipment rentals, dumpsters, PPE, demolition tools, dust control, and hazardous waste disposal. Labor is about 400 hours per 10,000 square feet.\n\nIf you liked this video, I appreciate the like and follow. If you're a contractor, come check out Contractor Plus. We've built the ultimate operating system for build and service contractors. Thanks for watching!",
