      embedUrl:
        "https://contractorplus.app/resources/contractor-costs/radon-mitigation-system-repair/national-average",
      transcript:
        "Did you know a broken radon mitigation system can put your home's air at risk? In this video, you'll learn how to repair a radon mitigation system step by step. \n\nFirst, turn off the system. Inspect the fan, PVC pipes, and all seals for cracks, leaks, or worn parts. Look for any signs of water damage or loose fittings. Identify which parts need replacing\u2014often it's the fan, some piping, or the seals.\n\nNext, replace the faulty fan and any damaged PVC pipes. Use new elbows and couplings as needed. Reseal all joints with urethane caulk to make sure no radon can escape. Check that all vent pipes are tight and secure. If any wiring is worn, replace it with new conduit and wire. Install a new system manometer if the old one is broken.\n\nOnce repairs are done, turn the system back on. Use a radon detector to check that the system is working and radon levels are safe. Make sure everything is running smoothly.\n\nFinally, clean up the area. Throw away old parts following your local rules. Write down what you fixed for your records.\n\nIf you liked this video, I appreciate the like and follow. If you're a contractor, come check out Contractor Plus. We've built the ultimate operating system for build and service contractors. Thanks for watching!",
