        "https://contractorplus.app/resources/contractor-costs/storm-drain-repair/national-average",
      transcript:
        "Ever wondered what it really takes to repair a storm drain the right way? In this video, you'll learn the step-by-step process to fix a damaged storm drain, from inspection to final restoration. \n\nFirst, start with a thorough inspection. Check for blockages, cracks, or collapsed sections. Set up safety barriers around the work area and gather all your tools and materials.\n\nNext, it's time for excavation. Carefully dig around the storm drain to expose the damaged pipe. Remove any debris and make sure the area is safe to work in. Use shoring or a trench box for safety if the trench is deep.\n\nNow, repair or replace the damaged sections. Cut out the bad pipe, install new 12-inch HDPE storm drain pipe, and use proper couplings and fittings. Secure all joints and, if needed, encase the pipe in concrete for extra strength. Replace any broken grates or inlets.\n\nAfter repairs, backfill the trench with gravel and soil. Compact each layer to prevent settling. Finally, restore the surface with asphalt or concrete to match the original look.\n\nIf you liked this video, I appreciate the like and follow. If you're a contractor, come check out Contractor Plus. We've built the ultimate operating system for build and service contractors. Thanks for watching!",
