      name: "How to Build a Raised Floor: Step-by-Step Guide",
      description:
        "Calculate materials & costs to build raised floor at https://contractorplus.app/resources/contractor-costs/build-raised-floor/national-average\n\nIn this video, we walk you through every step to build a sturdy raised floor, from planning and framing to installing supports and laying the subfloor. Learn how to measure your space, choose the right materials, and assemble a level, long-lasting floor structure. We cover essential tips for cutting lumber, using joist hangers, anchoring the frame, and ensuring your subfloor is squeak-free. Whether you\u2019re a DIY homeowner or a contractor, this guide will help you avoid common mistakes and get professional results. Like and follow for more how-to videos, and if you\u2019re a contractor, check out Contractor Plus for the best tools to run your business!",
      thumbnailUrl:
        "https://contractor-plus-website.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com/og-images/77e28353-6d0e-46a1-a78e-d1f6ef5afb30.jpg",
      uploadDate: "2025-08-04",
      duration: "PT2M17S",
      contentUrl:
        "https://contractor-plus-website.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com/reshub-how-to-vids/build-raised-floor.mp4",
      embedUrl:
        "https://contractorplus.app/resources/contractor-costs/build-raised-floor/national-average",
      transcript:
        "Want to build a solid raised floor that lasts for years? Let\u2019s break down the step-by-step process so you can do it right the first time. In this video, you\u2019ll learn how to plan, frame, support, and finish a raised floor for any space.\n\nFirst, measure your area and decide how high you want the floor. Gather all your materials: pressure-treated 2x6 lumber for joists and rim boards, subfloor plywood, deck blocks or piers, joist hangers, nails or screws, and construction adhesive. Cut your lumber to size before you start.\n\nNext, lay out your frame. Place the rim boards around the edges and install the joists every 16 inches on center. Use joist hangers for extra strength. Make sure the frame is square and level before you secure it to the existing floor or structure.\n\nNow, add blocking or extra supports between the joists. This keeps the floor from bouncing. Anchor the frame to the walls or footings, following your local building codes.\n\nTime for the subfloor. Lay 3/4-inch tongue and groove plywood sheets over the frame. Stagger the seams and fasten the panels with screws and adhesive. Walk the floor to check for squeaks and fix any loose spots.\n\nFinally, inspect your work. Fill any gaps, sand rough edges, and clean up. Your raised floor is now ready for the final flooring.\n\nIf you liked this video, I appreciate the like and follow. If you're a contractor, come check out Contractor Plus. We've built the ultimate operating system for build and service contractors. Thanks for watching!",
