      transcript:
        "Ever wondered what it really takes to repair an underground drain the right way? In this video, you'll learn the step-by-step process to fix a damaged underground drain, from start to finish. \n\nFirst, assess the site. Locate the damaged drain section, mark all utility lines, and get any permits you need. Safety comes first, so make sure the area is ready for digging.\n\nNext, it's time to excavate. Carefully dig to expose the drain pipe. Watch out for other utilities and try not to disturb the area more than needed.\n\nNow, repair or replace the pipe. Cut out the damaged section, clean the ends, and install new 4-inch PVC pipe with couplings and fittings. Use PVC primer and cement for a watertight seal. Once installed, test the pipe for leaks and make sure water flows as it should.\n\nAfter the repair, backfill the trench with gravel and soil. Compact it well and restore the surface. Don't forget to use trench safety tape and dispose of debris properly.\n\nIf you liked this video, I appreciate the like and follow. If you're a contractor, come check out Contractor Plus. We've built the ultimate operating system for build and service contractors. Thanks for watching!",
