      transcript:
        "Want to frame a non-load-bearing wall like a pro? In this video, I\u2019ll show you every step to build a strong, straight wall for your next project.\n\nFirst, gather your materials: 2x4 wood studs, pressure-treated bottom plates, 16d framing nails, construction adhesive, and sill sealer. You\u2019ll need about 34 studs and 2 plates for a 100-foot wall.\n\nStart with layout and marking. Measure and mark the wall location on the floor, ceiling, and nearby walls. Snap chalk lines for accuracy and check for any pipes or wires in the way.\n\nNext, cut your top and bottom plates to length. Lay them out on the floor, mark where each stud goes\u2014usually every 16 inches\u2014and mark any door or window openings. Assemble the plates and studs flat on the floor.\n\nNow, stand the wall frame up. Line it up with your chalk marks. Secure the bottom plate to the floor and the top plate to the ceiling. Use a level to make sure the wall is plumb, then nail the studs in place.\n\nAdd blocking where you\u2019ll need support for drywall or fixtures. Double-check all fasteners and make sure the wall is solid. Now you\u2019re ready for inspection or finishing!\n\nIf you liked this video, I appreciate the like and follow. If you're a contractor, come check out Contractor Plus. We've built the ultimate operating system for build and service contractors. Thanks for watching!",
