        "Ever wondered what it really takes to replace a drain line the right way? In this video, you'll learn the step-by-step process to remove and install a new 4-inch PVC drain line, plus what materials and tools you'll need. \n\nFirst, shut off the water supply and clear the work area. Protect floors and walls, then locate and expose the old drain line by removing any necessary flooring or wall sections. This usually takes about 2 to 3 hours.\n\nNext, disconnect any fixtures attached to the old line. Cut out the damaged pipe and safely remove all debris. Take a moment to inspect nearby pipes and fittings for any hidden issues. This step should take 3 to 4 hours.\n\nNow, it's time to install the new drain line. Measure and cut your 4-inch PVC pipe to fit. Use elbows and fittings as needed, and secure all joints with PVC cement and primer. Don't forget to add pipe hangers or straps for support, and use gravel or sand bedding if required. This part takes about 5 to 6 hours.\n\nFinally, restore the water supply and test the new drain line for leaks. Repair any removed flooring or walls, and clean up the area. This last step takes 2 to 3 hours.\n\nIf you liked this video, I appreciate the like and follow. If you're a contractor, come check out Contractor Plus. We've built the ultimate operating system for build and service contractors. Thanks for watching!",
