      transcript:
        "A burst pipe can flood your home in minutes\u2014do you know what to do? In this video, you'll learn how to handle an emergency pipe repair step by step, so you can stop the leak fast and avoid costly damage. \n\nFirst, shut off the main water supply right away. Find the leaking or burst pipe and check how bad the damage is. This helps you know what materials you\u2019ll need, like copper pipe, repair clamps, or fittings.\n\nNext, drain any leftover water from the line. Clean the area around the damaged pipe and remove any insulation or debris. You want a clear workspace to make the repair easier.\n\nNow, cut out the damaged section if needed. For small leaks, a repair clamp or epoxy putty might do the trick. For bigger breaks, use new pipe and couplings. Make sure all fittings are tight and match your plumbing type\u2014copper or PVC.\n\nOnce the repair is done, slowly turn the water back on. Watch the repair site for leaks and check water pressure throughout your home. If everything looks good, you\u2019re almost done.\n\nFinally, clean up the area and throw away any damaged pipe or insulation. Take photos and notes for your records or insurance.\n\nIf you liked this video, I appreciate the like and follow. If you're a contractor, come check out Contractor Plus. We've built the ultimate operating system for build and service contractors. Thanks for watching!",
