      transcript:
        "Did you know radon gas is the second leading cause of lung cancer in the US? Today, I\u2019ll show you how to design a radon mitigation system step by step, so you can keep your home or project safe. \n\nFirst, start with a site assessment. Test radon levels, check the foundation, and look for any spots where radon could enter. This takes about two hours, but it\u2019s crucial for planning.\n\nNext, plan your system layout. Decide where the radon fan and PVC piping will go. Make sure your discharge point meets local building codes. This step usually takes two to three hours.\n\nNow, select your components. You\u2019ll need a radon mitigation fan, 4-inch PVC piping, fittings, a U-tube manometer, roof flashing, caulk, electrical wiring, and gravel for sub-slab depressurization. Size everything based on your home\u2019s square footage and radon levels.\n\nAfter that, document your plan. Draw up the system, list your materials, and double-check that everything meets local codes and radon standards.\n\nFinally, review the design with your client. Answer their questions and finalize the plan.\n\nIf you liked this video, I appreciate the like and follow. If you're a contractor, come check out Contractor Plus. We've built the ultimate operating system for build and service contractors. Thanks for watching!",
