      transcript:
        "Want to turn your home theater into a true soundproof escape? In this video, I\u2019ll show you step-by-step how to soundproof a 200 square foot home theater room, so you get the best audio experience without disturbing the rest of your house.\n\nFirst, assess your room. Walk around and listen for spots where sound leaks\u2014like doors, windows, and thin walls. Choose the right materials: mineral wool batts for insulation, resilient channels, 5/8\" Type X drywall, acoustic sealant, putty pads, and door sweeps.\n\nStart with the walls and ceiling. Install acoustic insulation between studs, then attach resilient channels to decouple the drywall. Hang 5/8\" Type X drywall over the channels. Seal every seam and gap with acoustic caulk. Don\u2019t forget to use putty pads around electrical boxes.\n\nNext, upgrade your door to a solid core. Add a door sweep and perimeter seals. For windows, use soundproof curtains or window inserts.\n\nOn the floor, lay down a soundproof underlayment and add thick carpet or rugs to absorb vibrations.\n\nFinally, test your work. Play loud audio and listen for leaks. Seal any weak spots you find.\n\nIf you liked this video, I appreciate the like and follow. If you're a contractor, come check out Contractor Plus. We've built the ultimate operating system for build and service contractors. Thanks for watching!",
