        "https://contractorplus.app/resources/contractor-costs/replace-water-main/national-average",
      transcript:
        "Ever wondered what it really takes to replace a water main from start to finish? In this video, you'll learn the exact steps, materials, and time needed to replace a 1-inch copper water main, so you can plan your project with confidence. \n\nFirst, shut off the main water supply and notify everyone in the building. Find the existing water main and mark your dig area according to local utility rules. This usually takes 2 to 3 hours.\n\nNext, start excavation. Dig a trench along the route of the old pipe, expose it, and carefully remove it. Check for any obstructions and make sure your trench is deep enough to meet local codes. This step can take 10 to 12 hours.\n\nNow, install the new copper pipe. Connect brass fittings and the main shutoff valve, and secure all joints. Don\u2019t forget to pressure test the line for leaks before you cover it up. This part takes about 12 to 14 hours.\n\nBackfill the trench in layers, compact the soil, and restore any landscaping or pavement. This usually takes 8 to 9 hours.\n\nFinally, schedule a final inspection with your local authorities. Once approved, turn the water back on and check for any issues. This last step takes 2 to 3 hours.\n\nIf you liked this video, I appreciate the like and follow. If you're a contractor, come check out Contractor Plus. We've built the ultimate operating system for build and service contractors. Thanks for watching!",
