      transcript:
        "Did you know a damaged well cap can let in bugs and bacteria, risking your water? Today, I\u2019ll show you how to repair or replace a well cap the right way. \n\nFirst, shut off power to your well pump at the breaker. Safety first! Inspect the cap for cracks, loose bolts, or signs of dirt and insects. Next, use a wrench to carefully remove the cap. Clean both the cap and the top of the well casing\u2014get rid of any dirt or debris around the opening.\n\nIf you see minor cracks or a worn gasket, repair or replace those parts. If the cap is badly damaged, install a new one that fits tight and meets your local codes. Use stainless steel bolts and a sanitary sealant for a secure, watertight fit. \n\nFinally, reinstall the cap, tighten the bolts, and restore power to the pump. Check that everything runs smoothly. \n\nIf you liked this video, I appreciate the like and follow. If you're a contractor, come check out Contractor Plus. We've built the ultimate operating system for build and service contractors. Thanks for watching!",
