        "Is your floor squeaking or sagging? It might be time for a subfloor replacement! \n\nIn this video, you'll learn how to replace a subfloor step by step, so your floors are strong and ready for anything.\n\nFirst, remove the old flooring and underlayment. Take your time\u2014this usually takes 4 to 6 hours. Once it's up, inspect the joists and surrounding structure for any damage or moisture. Fix any issues before moving on.\n\nNext, cut out and remove the damaged subfloor sections. Clean the area well and check that the joists are level and solid. This prep work is key for a smooth install.\n\nNow, measure and cut your new 3/4-inch tongue and groove plywood panels. Stagger the seams for strength, and use construction adhesive plus subfloor screws to fasten each panel to the joists. This helps prevent squeaks and keeps everything tight.\n\nAfter the panels are down, seal the seams and reinforce the joints. Use shims or leveling compound if you find any uneven spots. Double-check for flatness before moving on.\n\nFinally, inspect your work. Make sure the new subfloor is stable and properly installed. Clean up all debris so you're ready for new flooring.\n\nIf you liked this video, I appreciate the like and follow. If you're a contractor, come check out Contractor Plus. We've built the ultimate operating system for build and service contractors. Thanks for watching!",
