        "https://contractorplus.app/resources/contractor-costs/collapsed-drain-repair/national-average",
      transcript:
        "Ever wondered what it really takes to fix a collapsed drain? Today, I\u2019ll show you the step-by-step process to repair a broken drain line, so you know exactly what\u2019s involved. \n\nFirst, assess the problem. Use a drain camera or probe to find the collapsed section. Clear the area for safe access\u2014this usually takes about 1 to 2 hours.\n\nNext, it\u2019s time to dig. Carefully excavate around the damaged pipe. Remove soil and debris until you can see the broken section. Take your time\u2014this step can take 3 to 4 hours.\n\nNow, remove the collapsed pipe. Measure and cut a new 4-inch PVC drain pipe to fit. Use PVC couplings and fittings to connect the new pipe to the existing line. Make sure the pipe is aligned and sloped correctly for proper drainage. Use PVC primer and cement to seal the joints. This replacement step also takes about 3 to 4 hours.\n\nBefore you cover it up, test the new pipe for leaks. Once it\u2019s good, backfill the trench with gravel and soil. Lay down trench safety tape to mark the repair. Restore the surface to match the original look. This final step takes 2 to 3 hours.\n\nIf you liked this video, I appreciate the like and follow. If you're a contractor, come check out Contractor Plus. We've built the ultimate operating system for build and service contractors. Thanks for watching!",
