      transcript:
        "Did you know a broken garage door spring can trap your car inside? Today, I\u2019ll show you how to safely replace a garage door torsion spring, step by step. \n\nFirst, always start with safety. Disconnect the power to your garage door opener. Clamp the door to the track so it can\u2019t move. Put on safety glasses and gloves, and gather your tools: winding bars, wrenches, and the new parts.\n\nNext, remove the old springs. Insert winding bars into the winding cone and carefully unwind the tension. Loosen the set screws, then slide the old springs and cables off the shaft. Take your time\u2014these springs are under a lot of force.\n\nNow, install the new torsion springs. Slide them onto the shaft, secure them with set screws, and reattach the cables and drums. Make sure everything lines up straight and tight.\n\nTime to add tension. Use the winding bars to wind the springs to the manufacturer\u2019s specs. Remove the clamps, reconnect the opener, and test the door. It should move smoothly and stay balanced.\n\nIf you liked this video, I appreciate the like and follow. If you're a contractor, come check out Contractor Plus. We've built the ultimate operating system for build and service contractors. Thanks for watching!",
