      embedUrl:
        "https://contractorplus.app/resources/contractor-costs/emergency-sump-pump-repair/national-average",
      transcript:
        "Is your basement flooding and your sump pump just quit? Don\u2019t panic\u2014here\u2019s how to fix it fast. In this video, you\u2019ll learn how to assess, repair, and test your sump pump in an emergency. \n\nFirst, unplug the sump pump and check for any visible damage or clogs in the pit. Make sure the float switch moves up and down freely\u2014debris can stop it from working. Clear out any gunk or obstructions you see.\n\nNext, disconnect the discharge pipe and carefully lift the pump out of the pit. Inspect the pump intake, impeller, and check valve for blockages or signs of wear. If the check valve is stuck or broken, replace it. Swap out any damaged pipes or fittings, and use silicone sealant to prevent leaks.\n\nNow, reinstall the pump and reconnect the discharge pipe. Plug the pump back in. Test it by pouring water into the pit\u2014watch to make sure it turns on and pumps water out smoothly. Check for leaks and confirm the discharge is working right.\n\nIf you liked this video, I appreciate the like and follow. If you're a contractor, come check out Contractor Plus. We've built the ultimate operating system for build and service contractors. Thanks for watching!",
