        "https://contractorplus.app/resources/contractor-costs/masonry-restoration/national-average",
      transcript:
        "Ever wondered how to restore old, damaged brickwork and make it look new again? In this video, you'll learn the step-by-step process for masonry restoration, from start to finish. \n\nFirst, assess your site. Walk around and inspect all masonry surfaces. Mark any damaged bricks or mortar joints. Set up protective barriers to keep the area safe, and gather your materials: Type N mortar, replacement bricks, masonry cleaner, backer rod, and joint sealant.\n\nNext, clean the surface. Use a low-pressure washer or a stiff brush to remove dirt, loose mortar, and debris. Make sure the area is clean so new mortar will bond well.\n\nNow, it's time for repairs. Mix your mortar according to the instructions. Fill any cracks and repoint the joints by packing fresh mortar into the gaps. Replace any bricks that are too damaged to save. Make sure new bricks match the old ones for a seamless look.\n\nAfter repairs, let the mortar cure. Mist the area with water if it\u2019s drying too fast. Once cured, clean off any extra mortar and touch up the surface. Apply joint sealant where needed to keep moisture out.\n\nIf you liked this video, I appreciate the like and follow. If you're a contractor, come check out Contractor Plus. We've built the ultimate operating system for build and service contractors. Thanks for watching!",
