      embedUrl:
        "https://contractorplus.app/resources/contractor-costs/sediment-filter-replacement/national-average",
      transcript:
        "Did you know a clogged sediment filter can ruin your water pressure and damage appliances? Today, I\u2019ll show you how to replace a sediment filter step by step, so your water stays clean and your system runs smooth.\n\nFirst, turn off the main water supply. Open a faucet to relieve pressure, and put a bucket or towel under the filter housing to catch any spills.\n\nNext, use a filter wrench to unscrew the housing. Carefully pull out the old filter. Clean the inside of the housing with warm water to get rid of any dirt or debris.\n\nNow, check the O-ring. If it\u2019s worn out, replace it. Add a little silicone grease to the O-ring to help it seal. Put in the new sediment filter, and screw the housing back on tight.\n\nSlowly turn the water supply back on. Watch for leaks around the housing. Open a faucet and let the water run for a few minutes to flush the system.\n\nThat\u2019s it! Your sediment filter is replaced and your water is protected.\n\nIf you liked this video, I appreciate the like and follow. If you're a contractor, come check out Contractor Plus. We've built the ultimate operating system for build and service contractors. Thanks for watching!",
