      transcript:
        "Ever wondered how you can fix a broken drain without digging up your whole yard? Today, I\u2019ll show you exactly how trenchless drain repair works, step by step. \n\nFirst, start with a site assessment. Use a camera to inspect the drain and find the damaged spots. Mark your access points and get any permits you need. This takes about 3 to 4 hours.\n\nNext, it\u2019s time for access and cleaning. Excavate small pits at the entry and exit points. Clean the pipe using hydro-jetting or mechanical tools. Make sure the pipe is spotless\u2014this is key for a good repair. This step usually takes 4 to 6 hours.\n\nNow, for the main event: trenchless pipe repair. Insert a Cured-in-Place Pipe liner soaked in epoxy resin through the old pipe. Inflate it, then cure it so it hardens into a new pipe inside the old one. This process takes 16 to 18 hours and creates a strong, seamless repair.\n\nFinally, inspect your work with a camera. Backfill the pits, restore any surfaces, and clean up the area. This last step takes 6 to 8 hours.\n\nIf you liked this video, I appreciate the like and follow. If you're a contractor, come check out Contractor Plus. We've built the ultimate operating system for build and service contractors. Thanks for watching!",
