      transcript:
        "Is your fence leaning or falling over? Replacing a fence post is easier than you think! In this video, you'll learn step-by-step how to remove an old fence post and install a new one that lasts. \n\nFirst, remove any fencing or hardware attached to the damaged post. Use a shovel to dig around the base and loosen the post. Pull it out of the ground\u2014this usually takes about 30 to 45 minutes.\n\nNext, clear out any debris from the hole. Make sure the hole is deep enough\u2014about one-third the length of your new 8-foot post, so around 2.5 feet deep. Pour in a layer of gravel for drainage. Place your new pressure-treated 4x4 post in the hole and use a level to make sure it's straight.\n\nNow, secure the post. Pour two 80-pound bags of concrete mix around the base, following the instructions on the bag. Double-check that the post stays level as the concrete sets. This takes about 45 to 60 minutes. Once the concrete is firm, reattach your fence panels and hardware using galvanized brackets.\n\nThat's it! Your fence is sturdy and ready to go. If you liked this video, I appreciate the like and follow. If you're a contractor, come check out Contractor Plus. We've built the ultimate operating system for build and service contractors. Thanks for watching!",
