      "@type": "VideoObject",
      name: "How to Repair Stone Mortar Joints Like a Pro",
      description:
        "Calculate materials & costs to stone mortar repair at https://contractorplus.app/resources/contractor-costs/stone-mortar-repair/national-average\n\nIn this video, we show you how to repair stone mortar joints step by step. Learn how to prep your work area, remove old mortar, mix and apply new mortar, and finish the job for a long-lasting, professional result. We cover the tools and materials you\u2019ll need, including Type N masonry mortar, matching stones, bonding agent, backer rod, and plastic sheeting. Whether you\u2019re a homeowner or a contractor, these tips will help you restore your stonework\u2019s strength and appearance. Have questions or want to share your own tips? Drop a comment below! Don\u2019t forget to like and follow for more how-to guides, and check out Contractor Plus for the best contractor tools and resources.",
      thumbnailUrl:
        "https://contractor-plus-website.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com/og-images/132dccce-9ee3-43ba-b765-08ee5aa9ffd6.jpg",
      uploadDate: "2025-08-04",
      duration: "PT2M7S",
      contentUrl:
        "https://contractor-plus-website.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com/reshub-how-to-vids/stone-mortar-repair.mp4",
      embedUrl:
        "https://contractorplus.app/resources/contractor-costs/stone-mortar-repair/national-average",
      transcript:
        "Cracked or crumbling mortar can ruin the look and strength of your stonework\u2014let\u2019s fix it right. \n\nIn this video, you\u2019ll learn how to repair stone mortar joints step by step, so your wall or patio looks solid and lasts for years.\n\nFirst, prep your work area. Lay down plastic sheeting to protect nearby surfaces. Use a chisel and brush to remove all loose or damaged mortar from the joints. Clean out the gaps well\u2014dust and debris will stop new mortar from sticking.\n\nNext, mix fresh Type N masonry mortar. Follow the bag instructions and add water slowly. You want a thick, peanut butter-like texture that sticks to your trowel but isn\u2019t runny.\n\nNow, fill the cleaned joints with mortar using a pointing trowel. Press the mortar in firmly, packing it tight. Shape the joints to match the old ones for a seamless look. If you need to replace any stones, set them in place with mortar and make sure they\u2019re level.\n\nFor wide joints, use a backer rod to fill deep gaps before adding mortar. This saves material and helps prevent cracks.\n\nOnce you\u2019re done, mist the joints with water and let the mortar cure for at least 8 hours. Wipe off any extra mortar from the stone faces before it hardens. Inspect your work for any missed spots.\n\nIf you liked this video, I appreciate the like and follow. If you're a contractor, come check out Contractor Plus. We've built the ultimate operating system for build and service contractors. Thanks for watching!",
