      embedUrl:
        "https://contractorplus.app/resources/contractor-costs/radon-sump-pump-installation/national-average",
      transcript:
        "Did you know a radon sump pump can protect your home from dangerous gas and water damage? In this video, you'll learn exactly how to install a radon sump pump system, step by step. \n\nFirst, assess your basement or crawl space. Find the best spot for the sump pit\u2014usually the lowest point. Check radon levels and get any needed permits. Gather your materials: a radon sump pump, sump basin, PVC and vent pipes, check valve, radon fan, wiring, and sealants.\n\nNext, excavate a pit deep enough for the sump basin. Place the basin in the hole and make sure it sits level. Backfill around it for stability. Now, install the sump pump inside the basin. Connect the 1.5-inch PVC discharge pipe and add a check valve to stop water from flowing back.\n\nSeal the pit with an airtight radon cover. Attach a 4-inch radon vent pipe and connect it to a radon fan. Run the vent pipe outside, above the roofline, to safely release radon. Wire the pump to a GFCI outlet for safety.\n\nFinally, patch any concrete you cut and seal all gaps with caulk. Test the pump and fan. Check for leaks and make sure radon levels drop. Inspect everything to meet local codes.\n\nIf you liked this video, I appreciate the like and follow. If you're a contractor, come check out Contractor Plus. We've built the ultimate operating system for build and service contractors. Thanks for watching!",
