      transcript:
        "Is your old central air conditioning unit costing you comfort and cash? In this video, you'll learn exactly how to replace a central air conditioning unit, step by step. \n\nFirst, shut down the power and recover any refrigerant from the old unit. Remove the old system and dispose of it safely, following local rules. Next, prep the site. Clean the area, check the ductwork, and make sure your electrical and drain lines are ready for the new install.\n\nNow, bring in your new 3-ton, 16 SEER unit. Set it in place, then connect the copper refrigerant lines, electrical disconnect, and sheet metal plenum. Hook up the new evaporator coil and programmable thermostat. Install the condensate drain line and make sure all connections are tight.\n\nVacuum the system, add the right amount of R410A refrigerant, and test for leaks. Calibrate the thermostat and run the system to check for proper cooling. Finally, clean up the work area, walk the homeowner through the new features, and share maintenance tips for your climate.\n\nIf you liked this video, I appreciate the like and follow. If you're a contractor, come check out Contractor Plus. We've built the ultimate operating system for build and service contractors. Thanks for watching!",
