      duration: "PT2M26S",
      contentUrl:
        "https://contractor-plus-website.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com/reshub-how-to-vids/water-heater-pilot-light-repair.mp4",
      embedUrl:
        "https://contractorplus.app/resources/contractor-costs/water-heater-pilot-light-repair/national-average",
      transcript:
        "Is your water heater not giving you hot water? The pilot light could be the problem. In this video, you'll learn how to safely inspect, clean, and relight your water heater's pilot light.\n\nFirst, turn off the gas supply and let the water heater cool for at least 15 minutes. Make sure the area is well-ventilated and gather your tools: a new thermocouple, flexible gas line sealant, and emery cloth.\n\nNext, remove the access panel. Look for dirt, debris, or any signs of gas leaks around the pilot assembly. If you smell gas, stop and call a professional.\n\nNow, use the emery cloth to gently clean the pilot orifice and thermocouple. This helps the flame stay steady. If the thermocouple looks damaged, replace it with a new one. Apply gas line sealant to any connections you loosened.\n\nFinally, follow your water heater\u2019s instructions to relight the pilot. Watch the flame\u2014it should be blue and steady. If it goes out again, you may need a new pilot assembly or more repairs.\n\nIf you liked this video, I appreciate the like and follow. If you're a contractor, come check out Contractor Plus. We've built the ultimate operating system for build and service contractors. Thanks for watching!",
