      "@type": "VideoObject",
      name: "How to Pressure Test a Gas Line: Step-by-Step Guide",
      description:
        "Calculate materials & costs to gas line pressure testing at https://contractorplus.app/resources/contractor-costs/gas-line-pressure-testing/national-average\n\nIn this video, learn how to safely and accurately pressure test a gas line, whether you\u2019re a contractor or a homeowner. We\u2019ll walk you through every step\u2014from shutting off the gas and inspecting lines, to setting up the pressure gauge, monitoring for leaks, and restoring the system. You\u2019ll also find out what tools and materials you need, how to use leak detection solution, and why documenting your results is crucial for code compliance. If you want to avoid dangerous gas leaks and ensure your system is safe, this guide is for you. Like and follow for more tips, and contractors\u2014discover how Contractor Plus can streamline your business!",
      thumbnailUrl:
        "https://contractor-plus-website.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com/og-images/c89ae79d-db3e-4645-ba75-ee4ec2f3dce8.jpg",
      uploadDate: "2025-08-04",
      duration: "PT2M54S",
      contentUrl:
        "https://contractor-plus-website.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com/reshub-how-to-vids/gas-line-pressure-testing.mp4",
      embedUrl:
        "https://contractorplus.app/resources/contractor-costs/gas-line-pressure-testing/national-average",
      transcript:
        "Did you know a simple pressure test can prevent dangerous gas leaks in your home or job site? Today, I\u2019ll show you exactly how to pressure test a gas line safely and correctly. \n\nFirst, shut off the gas supply and ventilate the area. Inspect all visible gas lines for damage or corrosion. Gather your tools: a pressure gauge, test plugs or caps, leak detection soap, and Teflon tape.\n\nNext, install the pressure gauge and test fittings on the gas line. Use an air compressor or hand pump to pressurize the system to the PSI required by your local code\u2014usually between 10 and 15 PSI, but always check your area\u2019s rules.\n\nNow, monitor the pressure gauge for at least an hour. Watch for any drop in pressure. While you wait, apply leak detection solution to all joints and connections. If you see bubbles, you\u2019ve found a leak\u2014tighten or repair as needed and retest.\n\nOnce the test is done, release the pressure safely, remove the test fittings, and restore the gas supply. Don\u2019t forget to document your results for code compliance.\n\nIf you liked this video, I appreciate the like and follow. If you're a contractor, come check out Contractor Plus. We've built the ultimate operating system for build and service contractors. Thanks for watching!",
